{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"The advantage comes from **how redemptions are funded**, not different stock returns:\n\n- An in-kind ETF delivers securities to redeeming authorized participants. Under **IRC §852(b)(6)**, a RIC generally recognizes no gain on that redemption distribution. Delivering low-tax-basis shares removes embedded gains without realizing them inside the fund.\n- A standalone cash-dealing mutual fund may sell appreciated stocks to pay redeeming shareholders. Net realized gains can then be distributed—and taxed—to shareholders who remain, even if they never sold fund shares.\n\n**Limits:** This generally defers investor tax, rather than eliminating it: selling ETF shares can trigger capital-gains tax, and dividends remain taxable. ETFs can still distribute gains from portfolio sales, rebalancing, or cash redemptions; loss offsets also matter. Mutual funds can use in-kind redemptions too—the mechanism, not the ETF label, matters. The advantage offers no current tax benefit inside tax-deferred or tax-exempt accounts."}}
